// `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=` need an ORDER, and only `Int`, `Float` and
// `String` have one. Anything else must be REJECTED by the checker.
//
// Nothing checked this until 2026-08-26, so every other type reached
// codegen and got whatever the generic emitter did with it: a
// comparison of ADDRESSES for anything heap-allocated. It was not a
// wrong answer so much as an incoherent one --
//
//     [1] < [2]   was true
//     [2] < [1]   was ALSO true
//
// -- which no ordering can satisfy. `Bool` had its own flavour:
// `icmp slt` on an `i1`, where `true` is -1 signed, so `true < false`
// came out true.
//
// Found while fixing the same bug one level down: ordered comparison
// of STRINGS compared addresses too, and the checker having no notion
// of which types are comparable is why nobody had ever asked the
// question.

struct P { x: Int }

let main (): Unit = {
    println(([1] < [2]).to_string());
}
